-- title:
--   Pull-backs of sections holomorphic on edge-region pieces lying over V
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ is a prime number and the following data are fixed.
--
--   **Base ring.** $\mathcal O$ is a commutative domain of characteristic zero together with the hypotheses `hdvr` ($\mathcal O$ is a discrete valuation ring), $\pi\in\mathcal O$ with `hπ` ($\pi$ irreducible), `hcomplete` ($\mathcal O$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete), `hres` (the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements) and `hunr` (the ideals $(r)$ and $(\pi)$ of $\mathcal O$ coincide, so $\mathcal O$ is absolutely unramified of residue characteristic $r$); $K_0$ is a field of characteristic zero which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra and a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Unramified coefficients.** $Onr$ is a commutative domain of characteristic zero which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra, $\mathrm{Fr}$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism of $Onr$, and the hypotheses are: `hOnr_complete` ($Onr$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$), `hOnr_max` (that ideal is maximal), `hOnr_alg` (every $x\in Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$), `hOnr_closed` (every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$, i.e. the residue field is algebraically closed) and `hFr` ($\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r}$ modulo $\pi$ for all $x$).
--
--   **Valuation of the determinant.** $v\!\det\colon GL_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is a group homomorphism, and `hvdet` states that $v\!\det(g)=n$ holds if and only if $\det g=u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Group datum.** $G$ is a group, $\sigma\colon G\to GL_2(K_0)$ and $\rho\colon G\to PGL_2(K_0)$ are homomorphisms with `hρ` ($\rho g$ is the class of $\sigma g$), and $\Gamma,\Gamma'\le G$ are subgroups subject to: `hcent` (some $z\in\Gamma$ has $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix and $v\!\det(\sigma z)=2$), `hodd` (some $w\in\Gamma$ has $v\!\det(\sigma w)=1$), `hΓ'` ($x\in\Gamma'$ exactly when $x\in\Gamma$ and $v\!\det(\sigma x)$ is even), `hdisc` (each vertex of the lattice tree of $\mathcal O$ in $K_0$ has finite stabiliser in $\rho(\Gamma')$) and `hcocpt` (a finite set of vertices meets every $\rho(\Gamma')$-orbit).
--
--   **Model.** $\mathcal X$ is a scheme in universe $0$ and $f\colon\mathcal X\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is proper and flat.
--
--   **Moduli interpretation.** $\Theta$ assigns, to each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map from pairs $(\psi,d)$ — $\psi$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $Onr\to B$ and $d$ a `DeligneDatum`, that is a choice, for every full lattice $M\subset K_0^2$, of a $B$-submodule $d(M)\subseteq B\otimes_{\mathcal O}M$ with invertible quotient, compatible with inclusions of lattices, equivariant for homotheties, and non-degenerate at every prime of $B$ in the sense of the `nondeg` clause — to the set of morphisms $\operatorname{Spec}B\to\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. It is subject to four hypotheses: `hΘnat` (naturality in $B$ along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps), `hΘinv` ($\Theta$ identifies pairs related by the twisted action of $\gamma\in\Gamma$, where `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` for $g$ requires $\psi'=\psi\circ\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\!\det(g)}$ and $d'$ to be the pullback of $d$ along $g^{-1}$), `hΘfib` (for every algebraically closed $\mathcal O$-field $k$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent and every $\psi\colon Onr\to k$, the slice $P\mapsto\Theta(\psi,P)$ is onto the $k$-points of $f$, and $\Theta(\psi,P)=\Theta(\psi,P')$ holds exactly when $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ are related by the twisted action of some $\gamma\in\Gamma$) and `hΘuniv` (the universal property: every natural, $\Gamma$-twist-invariant family $\rho'$ of points of a scheme $T$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ factors as $\rho'=u\circ\Theta$ for a natural family $u$ on the nilpotent points of $f$, and $u$ is unique among natural families with this factorisation).
--
--   **Analytic frame.** $C$ is an algebraically closed field with decidable equality, a $K_0$-algebra, valued with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$ and complete for that valuation; $R$ is a commutative ring which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra, $C$ is an $R$-algebra and an $\mathcal O$-algebra, and the towers $\mathcal O\to R\to C$ and $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$ commute. Furthermore $\varpi$ is a `PseudoUniformizer` of $K_0$ in $C$, i.e. an element $\varpi\in K_0$ with $0<v(\varpi)<1$ in $C$ and such that for each $a\in K_0^{\times}$ some power $v(\varpi)^{N}$ bounds $v(a)$ below and $v(\varpi)^{-N}$ bounds it above; `hF` asserts `IsAdicFrame π ϖ R`, that is: $\pi$ is irreducible, $R\to C$ is injective with image exactly the closed unit ball of $C$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ of valuation $\le 1$ in $C$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ is closed in $C$, and $\pi$ and $\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. In addition $\psi_0\colon Onr\to R$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra map, `hrk` states that for $x$ with $v(x)<1$ and $y\neq0$ some power $v(x)^{n}$ is $\le v(y)$, `hval` that every nonzero $\varepsilon\in\Gamma_0$ dominates $v(y)$ for some $y\neq0$ in $C$, `hex` that $\varpi$ is exhausting (`Omega.IsExhausted`: every $z\in C$ outside the image of $K_0$ lies in one of the affinoids $\mathrm{affinoid}(\varpi,n)$), and the ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ` of functions holomorphic on all these affinoids is assumed to be a domain.
--
--   **Uniformisation.** $\Phi$ sends each adic point $x$ — a compatible system of Deligne data over the rings $R/(\pi^{n+1})$ — to an $R$-point $\Phi x\colon\operatorname{Spec}R\to\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}R$, and `hΦ` has four clauses: the reduction of $\Phi x$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ equals $\Theta$ applied to the pair consisting of $\psi_0$ reduced modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ and the $n$-th component of $x$; $\Phi$ is surjective; $\Phi x=\Phi x'$ holds if and only if $x'=x.\mathrm{act}(\sigma\gamma)$ for some $\gamma\in\Gamma'$; and base change along $R\to C$ is injective on such $R$-points and hits every $C$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}C$. Finally $s_C\colon\operatorname{Spec}C\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is the morphism induced by $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$ (hypothesis `hsC`), and the pullback of $f$ along $s_C$ is assumed integral.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $g\in GL_2(K_0)$, every open $V\subseteq\mathcal X$ and every section $p\in\mathcal O_{\mathcal X}(V)$ there are a natural number $m$ and two families $L,M\colon\mathrm{Fin}\,m\to\mathrm{Finset}(C\times C)$ with the following four properties. Write $\bar g$ for the class of $g$ in $PGL_2(K_0)$, $\mathrm{flip}=\mathrm{diag}(\varpi,1)$ for `edgeFlip K₀ ϖ`,
--   $$T_g=\mathrm{vertexTube}(\varpi,\bar g)\cup\mathrm{edgeTube}(\varpi,\bar g)\cup\mathrm{vertexTube}(\varpi,\overline{g\cdot\mathrm{flip}})$$
--   for the closed edge region attached to $g$ (a vertex tube being the set of $z\in C\setminus K_0$ whose image under the Möbius map of the inverse class lies in $\mathrm{affinoid}(\varpi,0)$, an edge tube the corresponding condition for the standard edge tube $v(\varpi)<v(\cdot)<1$), and for each $k$
--   $$S_k=\{z\in T_g:\ v(r)\le v(u(z)-e)\ \text{for all }(e,r)\in L_k,\quad v(u(z)-e)\le v(r)\ \text{for all }(e,r)\in M_k\},$$
--   where $u(z)=\mathrm{pmoebius}(\bar g^{-1})\,z$ is the chart coordinate.
--
--   (1) For every $k$, the second coordinate of each pair in $L_k$ is nonzero, and likewise for $M_k$.
--
--   (2) For every adic point $x$ and every $z\in C$ with $z=x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ (the unique $z$ with $(z,1)$ in the $C$-line of $x$, and $0$ if there is no such unique element): if $z\in T_g$ and the whole of $\operatorname{Spec}R$ lies in the preimage $(\Phi x)^{-1}V$, then $z\in S_k$ for some $k$.
--
--   (3) For every $k$, every adic point $x$ and every $z=x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ lying in $S_k$, the whole of $\operatorname{Spec}R$ lies in $(\Phi x)^{-1}V$; that is, each piece lies over $V$.
--
--   (4) For every $k$ there is a function $F\colon S_k\to C$ belonging to $\mathrm{holOn}\,C\,S_k$ — a uniform limit on $S_k$ of a uniformly bounded sequence of quotients of polynomial pairs which are pole-free on $S_k$ — such that for every adic point $x$ and every $z\in S_k$ with $z=x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$, and for every witness that $\operatorname{Spec}R$ lies in $(\Phi x)^{-1}V$, the value $F(z)$ is the image in $C$ of the element of $R$ obtained from $p$ by pulling back along $\Phi x$, restricting to the whole of $\operatorname{Spec}R$ along that inclusion of opens, and using the identification of $\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec}R,\mathcal O)$ with $R$.
--
--   This is a step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation chain: it says that the pull-back along the $p$-adic uniformisation $\Phi$ of a section of the structure sheaf of the model $\mathcal X$ is, on each of finitely many pieces of a closed edge region cut out by finitely many valuation inequalities in the chart coordinate, the restriction of a rigid-analytic (holomorphic) function, the pieces being chosen so that each of them lies entirely over the open set $V$ on which the section is defined. It is used in the construction of the evaluation map on the function field of the base change of $\mathcal X$ to $C$ and in the chartwise meromorphy of pulled-back rational functions.
